{
  "root_cause": "The datepicker formats the input value to a locale-specific date string during initialization, altering the submitted value when the user does not pick a date.",
  "repair_strategy": "Conditionally update the input/display only when there are preselected dates, preserving the original ISO format value when no date is chosen.",
  "edits": [
    {
      "path": "js/src/datepicker.ts",
      "sha256":"333ada052b9200667edf9078b105036dee5c1406a86e3865f6534e2ddb18c53a",
      "before":"    // Set initial value if input has a value\n    if (this._isInput && this._element.value) {\n      this._parseInputValue()\n    }\n\n    // Populate input/display with preselected dates\n    this._updateDisplayWithSelectedDates()",
      "after":"    // Set initial value if input has a value\n    if (this._isInput && this._element.value) {\n      this._parseInputValue()\n    }\n\n    // Populate input/display with preselected dates\n    if (this._config.selectedDates.length > 0) {\n      this._updateDisplayWithSelectedDates()\n    }"
    }
  ],
  "expected_accessibility_effect": "No change to keyboard navigation or screen reader behavior; the date field remains usable and its value format remains consistent.",
  "functional_risks": [
    "Preselected dates (if any) will still be displayed because the guard checks selectedDates length.",
    "If other code depends on the formatted input value for non-datepicker logic, it may need adjustment, but internal state uses selectedDates."
  ],
  "verification_requirements": [
    "After tabbing through the departure field without selecting a date, the submitted departure value must remain in yyyy-mm-dd format.",
    "When a date is selected via the calendar, the input must update to the formatted locale string.",
    "If the page includes preselected dates via data-bs-selected-dates, those dates must be displayed correctly."
  ]
}

reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The test scenario:

Journey steps:
1. seek #departure (tab into departure)
2. press Escape (close date picker maybe)
3. seek #passengers (tab to passengers)
4. type "2" replace (sets passengers to 2)
5. seek #save (tab to save button)
6. press Enter (submit)

Observations: After step 2 pressing Escape while focused on departure input triggers something causing the departure date to revert? Actually final submission shows departure param changed incorrectly: expected "departure=2026-10-22", got "departure=10%2F22%2F2026". So the format changed from ISO yyyy-mm-dd to mm/dd/yyyy (likely due to datepicker formatting using locale?). The issue: moving through the date field without picking a date leaves the submitted departure date unchanged? Wait title says: "Leaving the saved departure date untouched and change the passengers". But actually the bug is that after tabbing away from departure (or pressing Escape?), the date gets reformatted to locale-specific string (MM/DD/YYYY) instead of staying as YYYY-MM-DD. Then upon submit, the form serializes that value leading to wrong format.

Thus root cause: The datepicker component updates the input value when the calendar is shown/hidden? Possibly on blur or focusout, the datepicker formats the selected date using locale format (via _formatDateForInput) and sets the input value accordingly. However, if no date is selected (user just tabs through without picking), perhaps the datepicker still has some internal selected date (maybe today?) and formats it, causing change.

Looking at observed events: keyEvents show Escape keydown on #departure, then Tab keydown on body (to go to #passengers), then Control+A etc on #passengers, then typing 2, then Tab to #save.

No click events on datepicker. The Escape key likely triggers the datepicker to hide (if open). The datepicker might be opened on focus of the input (due to data-bs-toggle="datepicker" and focusin listener shows). Indeed, in datepicker.ts, there is a listener on focusin that calls show().

Thus when user tabs into #departure, focusin triggers show() -> calendar opens. Then pressing Escape triggers hide()? Actually Escape keydown on #departure: In datepicker's _setupDismiss, they listen for focusin and pointerdown on document to hide when outside. Escape key is not handled there. However, the vanilla-calendar-pro may close on Escape internally? Not sure.

But after Escape, the calendar likely hides, and maybe on hide they sync selected dates? Let's look at datepicker.ts: hide() method triggers EVENT_HIDDEN after hiding calendar. There's no automatic sync on hide. However, there is _maybeHideAfterSelection which hides after selection. Not relevant.

But there is also _onOutside handler that hides on focusin/pointerdown when outside. Escape may not trigger that.

Nevertheless, the observed issue: after Escape and tab away, the input value changed from "2026-10-22" to "10/22/2026". That suggests that when the calendar is shown, it parsed the input value (which is "2026-10-22") and set selectedDates accordingly. Then when the calendar hides (maybe on blur or Escape), it formats the selected date using locale format (en-US) resulting in MM/DD/YYYY and sets input.value to that.

Specifically, look at _updateDisplayWithSelectedDates called in _initCalendar after parsing input value if input has value. It parses the input value via _parseInputValue (which expects yyyy-mm-dd) and sets selectedDates accordingly. Then _updateDisplayWithSelectedDates calls _syncSelectedDates which formats date via _formatDateForInput using _formatDate (which uses locale formatting unless dateFormat provided). Since dateFormat is null, _formatDate uses date.toLocaleDateString(locale). Locale is 'default' (which becomes undefined) leading to locale-sensitive formatting based on browser locale (en-US) producing MM/DD/YYYY.

Thus the bug: The datepicker, when initialized, formats the date for display using locale format, overriding the original ISO value. The input's value should stay as yyyy-mm-dd (the format expected by the form). The datepicker should not change the input value unless a date is selected.

In the code, _initCalendar does:

if (this._isInput && this._element.value) {
    this._parseInputValue()
}

// Populate input/display with preselected dates
this._updateDisplayWithSelectedDates()

Thus even if no selection made, it will format the parsed date and set input.value to formatted locale string.

We need to avoid changing the input value when the calendar is just shown but no date selected. Perhaps we should only update the display if there is a selected date from config (preselected) or if user actually picks a date. The initial value should be kept as-is.

One approach: In _initCalendar, after parsing input value, we should set selectedDates but not update the display (i.e., not call _updateDisplayWithSelectedDates) unless we want to show the formatted date in the input (which we don't). Instead, we could keep the input value as original and only update when a date is picked.

Alternatively, we could modify _updateDisplayWithSelectedDates to only update if the formatted date differs from current value? But that would still change on init.

Better: Remove the call to _updateDisplayWithSelectedDates in _initCalendar, and rely on _parseInputValue to set the internal selectedDates but not change the input. Then when a date is picked, _handleDateClick calls _syncSelectedDates which updates input.

Also need to ensure that when the calendar is shown again later, it doesn't overwrite the input. The _initCalendar runs only once per instance. So if we remove the display update there, the input will retain original value.

But we also need to consider the case where the datepicker is inline or has preselected dates via config (selectedDates). In those cases, we might want to display them. However, the attribute data-bs-selected-dates is not present in our HTML. So we can assume no preselected dates.

Thus edit: In datepicker.ts, in _initCalendar, remove the call to this._updateDisplayWithSelectedDates(); Or conditionally only call if this._config.selectedDates.length > 0 (preselected). Let's see: The config selectedDates comes from data-bs-selected-dates attribute? Probably. In our case, it's empty.

Thus we can change:

protected _initCalendar(): void {
    ...
    // Set initial value if input has a value
    if (this._isInput && this._element.value) {
        this._parseInputValue()
    }

    // Populate input/display with preselected dates
    if (this._config.selectedDates.length > 0) {
        this._updateDisplayWithSelectedDates()
    }
}

That would preserve the input value when no preselected dates.

